{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785595326,"nonce":"YhUmssjYx3qn2j1BaGzStoqA7Jo=","message":"**Stage 1 landed at `e58ce1f` — the DRAIN half of this ticket is done; the FEED half is staged, not built. Ticket stays OPEN for stage 2.**\n\n**What changed (drain):** the exec-tar tunnel, the CP tempdir, and CP-side ingest/hash are deleted. The egress sidecar is the wsfetch image (`hold`); the CP execs `scarab-wsfetch drain` (argv in, exit code out); the helper ingests to Depot warm, prunes+folds identity in-process, POSTs a DrainRecord; the CP GETs it by fence_key, flushes (ADR-0064 unchanged, still gates Succeeded), patches annotations, releases. \"Server exchanges root hashes only\" is now literally true for the drain.\n\n**What the reviews forced, worth reading before stage 2:**\n- A per-fence write ledger on the Depot. The red team proved cross-fence hash-naming exfiltration was REAL: nothing binds a tree hash to a run on the read path, and this design was about to let the stamped string arrive over HTTP from a Pod. The ledger + server-side closure validation on the record POST closes it. Ledger appends ONLY on actual tree PUT (never /have — a /have-ledger launders foreign hashes); drains PUT trees unconditionally for this reason; the ledger read-arm never authorizes /flat.\n- Record-first classification: exit codes ride a droppable status frame (git-bug a0b42ad). Exit 0 with no record = the stale-image signature → prompt Config naming the skew.\n- fence_key GETs, because invoke-namespaced step ids contain `/`.\n- The egress container runs as root deliberately (busybox always did) — a 0600 file must not silently vanish.\n\n**Checklist against the original ticket:**\n- [x] Drain: Scarab-owned helper pushes blobs straight to the workspace service\n- [x] busybox doorstops (WORKSPACE_HELPER_IMAGE) retired\n- [x] Server receives one root hash per Attempt (drain leg)\n- [ ] Feed: the eager wsfetch init container still copies the whole snapshot; the lazy Export/NFS/PVC feed is ADR-0062 stage 2 — build-order table now IN ADR-0062 (ganesha spike → colima nfs client → PV/PVC lifecycle → fsGroup → 0ad393c overlay test → eager|lazy cutover). NOTE: stage 2 cannot be verified on this dev machine (colima lacks an NFS client; the overlay path needs privileged Linux) — minimum viable env is one Linux box running kind.\n- [ ] `scarab-workspace-init` disappears — only with the feed leg.\n\n**Follow-ups filed from review:** 0d3666a (artifacts ride the Depot — the LAST exec-tar), 1d4b3ce (closure validation is a serial walk on the critical path), 16a7768 (egress resources / non-regular files / 24h token expiry / ledger growth). Not yet live-verified on the kind tier (`just kube-tests`).","files":null}]}